# Line endings are decided here rather than by whoever cloned the repository.
#
# .editorconfig has said `end_of_line = crlf` since the beginning and nothing
# enforced it: an editor obeys it, and a file written by a tool, a generator or a
# container does not. The result was 539 files with LF and 24 with CRLF, none of
# it deliberate and all of it invisible until a diff showed every line of a file
# nobody had touched.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text and normalise it to LF *in the
# repository*, which is what keeps a diff readable and a merge possible.
# `eol=crlf` is what lands on disk, so the working tree matches .editorconfig and
# Visual Studio on Windows. The two are not in conflict — they describe different
# places, and the file you edit is the second one.
* text=auto eol=crlf

# Shell scripts are the exception and it is not cosmetic: a CRLF in a script with
# a shebang makes the interpreter part of the argument, and the error is
# "/bin/bash^M: bad interpreter".
*.sh text eol=lf

# Left alone entirely. Git detects most of these anyway; naming them means a
# corrupted PNG can never be blamed on a newline conversion.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.dll binary
*.snk binary
